\paragraph{Representational diversity.}
CKA similarity between dense and pruned representations (Figure~\ref{fig:cka_heatmap}) shows CKA-only diverges most from the dense model ($\mu{=}0.62$), while Standard ($\mu{=}0.90$), Random ($\mu{=}0.92$), and \rasp{} ($\mu{=}0.88$) cluster near 1.0.
Preserving dense fidelity through gradient importance appears to retain redundant representations at the expense of the diversity that GRPO exploration requires.
CKA-only concentrates pruning in early layers (L0--9, 72.5\% of removed groups) while Standard prunes uniformly, removing late-layer structures that are representationally unique (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_overlap}).
Gradient flow analysis corroborates this: CKA-only models exhibit 2.89$\times$ mean gradient magnitude versus Standard across 55/56 layer groups ($p{<}0.05$, Wilcoxon; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_gradient}), consistent with diversity preservation enabling stronger RL training signals.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig5_extended_training.pdf}
    \caption{Extended GRPO training (300 steps, 3$\times$ budget). (a)~Standard reward: zero net gain (0.054$\to$0.056); CKA-only sustains a 5.5$\times$ gap. (b)~Standard $f_0$ remains in the collapse zone ($>$0.5); CKA-only stays below 0.30.}
    \label{fig:extended_training}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Extended training cannot rescue collapse.}
Extending GRPO from 100 to 300 steps (Figure~\ref{fig:extended_training}), Standard shows zero net improvement (reward 0.054--0.058, $f_0 > 0.5$ throughout), while CKA-only sustains a 5.5$\times$ gap (0.308 vs 0.056 at step 300). The gap is structural, not a matter of training budget.

\paragraph{Cross-scale and cross-architecture generalization.}
On DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B at 20\% sparsity (Table~\ref{tab:cross_scale}), the three-method ordering is preserved (CKA-only 35.48\% $\gg$ \rasp{} 26.16\% $>$ Standard 22.29\%), with reward-accuracy decoupling confirmed at this scale ($|\Delta| < 1$\,pp: CKA-only $-$0.15, Standard $-$0.61\,pp).
On Qwen2.5-Math-7B at 20\% sparsity (Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch}, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_cross_arch}), CKA-only causes near-total initial damage (1.0\% vs Standard's 18.5\%) yet SFT recovers it to 51.50\% $\pm$ 3.53 while Standard remains at 21.00\% $\pm$ 2.88 ($N{=}200$), confirming that recoverability depends on preserved structure, not starting accuracy. GRPO validation on this architecture is left to future work.

We note that \citet{selfhealing2025} evaluate unstructured magnitude pruning retaining 96.5\% of dense parameters; our findings concern fine-grained structural pruning where entire channel groups are removed, and the two settings may impose different demands on RL recovery.

